<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Ink, not accent.

  This was the app's blue, on the reasoning that the icon and the app should be the same
  object. On a home screen it read as one more blue tile among a dozen, and with themed
  icons switched on it was the *only* place the blue survived — so the launcher showed a
  wallpaper-tinted mark and everywhere else showed a blue one, which is two identities
  for one app.

  Near-black is what the app's own ground is, and it lets the white clasp carry the mark
  by itself. It is also the closest a fixed icon gets to the themed one, so the two read
  as the same drawing rather than as a pair.

  Not pure #000: against a black wallpaper the edge would vanish entirely, and the mask's
  shape is part of the recognition.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:pathData="M0,0h108v108h-108z"
        android:fillColor="#FF0F0F14" />
</vector>
